<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Ravensberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1140 CE – 1807 CE (approx.) · capital Bielefeld</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Westphalian county around Bielefeld that joined the Jülich-Cleves inheritance and became a Prussian linen-weaving region.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Ravensberg appear around 1140, named after a castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgholzhausen">Borgholzhausen</a>. Their territory grew between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> hills, and around 1214 Count Hermann IV founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparrenburg">Sparrenburg</a> castle.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ravensberg became known for linen weaving, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> a centre of the trade. In 1346 the county passed by marriage to the counts of Berg and later formed part of the united territory of Jülich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> and Berg. Its estates kept local institutions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession">Jülich succession crisis</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> of 1614 assigned Ravensberg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, confirmed in 1666. It remained Prussian, was briefly part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> from 1807, and then joined the Prussian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-ravensberg.jpg" alt="County of Ravensberg" width="320" height="400"><figcaption>Image: Willem Janszoon Blaeu and Joan Blaeu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grafschaft_Ravensberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1140 CE – 1807 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bielefeld</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermann IV of Ravensberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ravensberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1474&amp;focus=county-of-ravensberg" aria-label="County of Ravensberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4873 378 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Ravensberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5193 634L5229 694M5193 634L5154 659M5193 634L5282 625M5193 634L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5154 659h0M5282 625h0M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5193 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5193 634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1474&amp;focus=county-of-ravensberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
